  "summary": "The previous pragmatic incremental approach of gating existing sequential blocks in _run_pipeline_impl has three critical weaknesses. First, it doesn't solve the data contract problem it claims to sidestep: the impl/test/review block consumes impl_spec and test_spec produced by spec_writing (phases.py line 1574-1609), so skipping spec writing without providing an alternative data source means the implementing phase receives empty specs and produces low-quality output -- the 'gate check' approach just moves the item past a block without filling the fields downstream blocks depend on. Second, the hardcoded stage list appears in at least four places (config.py lines 121-124 in ensure_local_config, lines 540-543 in setup_boards, lines 557-558 in add_board, plus the PIPELINE_PHASES constant at line 37), not counting the WebUI and TUI rendering logic. The incremental approach papers over _run_pipeline_impl but leaves all these other consumers of the stage list untouched, creating inconsistency between what the pipeline executes and what the filesystem/UI presents. Third, and most fundamentally, the error-handling escape hatches throughout the pipeline (e.g., moving to final-human-approval on exhausted retries at lines 1504-1508, 1539-1546, 1566-1572) assume final-human-approval always exists as a valid destination -- a board that skips this stage would hit runtime errors on these paths. The right next step is not choosing between 'refactor first' and 'gate checks': it's defining a minimal interface that each block must satisfy (input fields required, output fields produced, valid error-escape stages), then validating board configurations against those contracts at startup time rather than discovering incompatibilities at runtime."
